Falsifies `system_tests::user::x86_port_tests::a_holder_that_deletes_its_port_capability_faults_on_its_next_port_write`.

Architecture: x86_64

The defect is the one milestone 313's audit found in the tree on 2026-09-17, put back: a thread's
`SYS_CAP_DELETE` of its own `PortRange` capability clears the capability table and leaves
`Thread::port_range_grant` alone. The context switch installs that cached grant into the TSS I/O
bitmap on every switch-in, and nothing else consults the table, so the thread keeps `in`/`out`
access to the ports for the rest of its life. §12 says a consumed capability cannot be used again;
this is that claim failing for the one object enforced outside the table.

The patch removes the two things the fix added to `sched::delete_current_cap` (clearing the cache,
and resetting this core's bitmap, both under the lock since milestone 315) and keeps the lookup, so the only thing
that changes is whether the grant survives the delete. `delete_port_range_caps_impl`, the
`REVOKE` path, is untouched: `a_revoked_holder_faults_on_its_next_port_write` stays green under
this patch, which is the point. The two milestone 299 tests prove the grant follows the capability
across a switch and across a revoke, and neither can see this defect.

Expected to fail: `assert_eq!(msg[0], EVENT_FAULT, "a holder that deleted its own port capability
must fault on its next `out`; it kept the ports after dropping the capability")`, holding
`EVENT_EXIT` (2) where it wanted `EVENT_FAULT` (1). The child's `out` is permitted, it reaches its
`SYS_EXIT`, and the supervisor's message is a clean exit.

**Why the child exits rather than reporting, recorded because the first draft did not.** With
[`port_out`]'s `SEND`-then-exit tail, this defect did not turn the test red: the permitted `out` was
followed by a `SEND` on a rendezvous the test was not receiving (it was blocked on the supervision
endpoint waiting for a death report), the child parked forever, and the run hung until the harness's
bound. That is `notes/confinement-claims.md`'s row 26 (a real escape hangs the run) one object
over, and it was met on the first replay of this record. The program was changed to exit after the
`out`, which makes the escape arrive as `EVENT_EXIT` in the assertion that states the claim.

Replayed by hand on 2026-09-17 with the sweep's own argument list (`cargo xtask test --arch
x86_64 --test a_holder_that_deletes`), red at `system_tests/src/user/x86_port_tests.rs`, `left: 2`,
`right: 1`.

Regenerated 2026-09-25 (UTC) against 53cb6d620, which landed
milestone 315 (a port revoke that reaches every core). That commit moved the bitmap reset inside the `IPC_TABLES` critical section and
rewrote the comment above it, so the old hunk no longer matched. Same two removals, same defect, new
